Consumer Loan Default Risk: Predictive Analytics & Underwriting Decision-Support Report
Prepared For: Retail Credit Risk & Underwriting Leadership | Dataset: CSE bank data project 1.csv (n = 252,000) | Deployment: Human-in-the-Loop Streamlit Scoring Application

Page 1: Business Problem, Dataset Profile & Exploratory Insights
1. Business Problem & Analytical Objective
Consumer lending portfolios face a structural trade-off between credit loss prevention and applicant approval volume. 
When default incidence is concentrated within a minority segment of borrowers, manual underwriting teams struggle to separate high-risk applicants from creditworthy borrowers using single-variable rules alone, while rigid automated rejections risk turning away 
viable customers (false positives). The objective of this Business Intelligence initiative is to identify key behavioral, demographic, and socioeconomic drivers of consumer loan default (Risk_Flag) and deploy a validated, interpretable machine learning decision-support tool that prioritizes human underwriting review rather than automating credit rejections.

2. Dataset Overview & Integrity Audit
The source dataset (CSE bank data project 1.csv) comprises 252,000 consumer loan applications across 13 attributes with zero missing values:

Target Variable (Risk_Flag): Binary default indicator exhibiting substantial class imbalance—221,004 non-default applications (87.70%) versus 30,996 default applications (12.30%).

Numeric Attributes (5 Features): Annual Income (mean 4,997,117, median 5,000,695, SD 2,878,311, range 10,310–9,999,938), Age (mean 49.95 yrs, median 50.0 yrs, range 21–79), total Experience (mean 10.08 yrs, range 0–20), CURRENT_JOB_YRS (mean 6.33 yrs, range 0–14), and CURRENT_HOUSE_YRS (mean 12.00 yrs, range 10–14).

Categorical Attributes (6 Features) & Identifier (Id): Married/Single (2 levels), House_Ownership (3 levels), Car_Ownership (2 levels), Profession (51 roles), STATE (29 states), CITY (317 raw labels), and sequential row index Id (1–252,000). Across all 252,000 records, there are 42,007 unique borrower feature profiles reflecting recurring demographic/occupational cohorts.

3. Top Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) Insights
Early Current-Job Tenure Is the Sharpest Behavioral Risk Signal: Borrowers in their current job for 0 to 2 years default at 15.65% (peaking at 17.05% in year 2), compared to 11.78% for borrowers with 3 or more years of current job tenure—a +3.87 percentage-point elevation in default risk.

Young & Early-Career Borrowers Exhibit Higher Vulnerability: Applicants aged 21–30 experience a 14.47% default rate versus 11.69%–12.06% across older age brackets (31–79). Similarly, applicants with 0–2 years of total work experience default at 15.65%, declining steadily to 10.95% among borrowers with 16–20 years of experience.

Asset Ownership & Household Stability Buffer Default Risk: Renters (92.0% of applicants) default at 12.56% compared to 8.98% among homeowners (-3.58 percentage points). Single applicants default at 12.53% versus 10.25% for married applicants, and non-car owners default at 12.82% versus 11.10% for car owners.

High Dispersion Across Professions and Geographies: Default rates vary more than twofold across occupations—from 16.41% (Police_officer), 15.36% (Chartered_Accountant), and 15.21% (Army_officer) down to 8.15% (Technology_specialist) and 8.51% (Petroleum_Engineer). Across states with 
