4. Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering
To prevent data leakage and expose non-linear risk patterns to predictive algorithms, a reproducible transformation pipeline was constructed:

Leakage & Noise Removal: Dropped the sequential Id column to prevent tree splits from memorizing row order, and stripped bracketed reference artifacts (\[\d+\]) from 30 CITY labels (e.g., Kota[6] 
→
→ Kota, consolidating 317 raw strings into 316 canonical cities).

Domain Feature Engineering: Created four financial and career-stability ratios:

Income_per_Age (Income / Age): Normalizes earning power relative to borrower life stage.

Job_to_Exp_Ratio (CURRENT_JOB_YRS / Experience): Measures employer retention relative to total career length (0.0 when Experience == 0).

Exp_to_Age_Ratio (Experience / Age): Captures workforce attachment consistency.

Stability_Score (CURRENT_JOB_YRS + CURRENT_HOUSE_YRS): Composite index of employment and residential permanence.

Partitioning & Encoding: Applied a stratified 80/20 split (201,600 development rows for cross-validation; 50,400 untouched test rows). Linear models received StandardScaler + OneHotEncoder, while tree-based models used OneHotEncoder for low-cardinality fields 
and OrdinalEncoder (with unknown-category fallback -1) for Profession, CITY, and STATE. All models incorporated balanced class/sample weighting to counter the 87.7% / 12.3% class imbalance.

